{"parentUuid":"b6dd350e-79ff-4a7b-b017-f637e544a528","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"msg_c64c2624f713416d92377415","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"mimo-v2.5-pro","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_50c5ff0405c440638a1f2212","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/warner/openvela/nuttx/Documentation/platforms/arm/stm32n6/boards/nucleo-n657x0-q/index.rst"}}],"usage":{"input_tokens":401,"output_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":43712}},"type":"assistant","uuid":"d719d8a4-e8ac-47e6-90d9-c0c3a3b27100","timestamp":"2026-08-20T09:19:52.717Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/warner/openvela","sessionId":"4e5d5a04-986e-4343-ae7e-1f641f6267d8","version":"2.1.237","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"d719d8a4-e8ac-47e6-90d9-c0c3a3b27100","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"2a8eeb49-4773-4916-8fe1-4e2a8a39eaf2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_50c5ff0405c440638a1f2212","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t==================\n2\tST Nucleo-N657X0-Q\n3\t==================\n4\t\n5\t.. tags:: chip:stm32, chip:stm32n6, chip:stm32n657\n6\t\n7\t.. figure:: nucleo-n657x0-q.jpg\n8\t   :width: 50 %\n9\t   :align: center\n10\t   :alt: STMicro Nucleo-N657X0-Q development board\n11\t\n12\t   Nucleo-N657X0-Q\n13\t\n14\tThe STM Nucleo-N657X0-Q is a development\n15\tboard for the STM32N657X0H3Q, an Arm Cortex-M55 with 4 MiB of on-chip\n16\tAXISRAM, an on-chip Neural-ART NPU, and no internal flash. In\n17\tproduction, code is fetched from external XSPI NOR flash and in\n18\tdevelopment, the image is loaded directly into AXISRAM by the on-board\n19\tST-LINK-V3EC. Refer to the\n20\thttps://www.st.com/en/evaluation-tools/nucleo-n657x0-q.html website\n21\tfor the full product page.\n22\t\n23\tFeatures\n24\t========\n25\t\n26\t* STM32N657X0H3Q MCU (Arm Cortex-M55, up to 800 MHz)\n27\t* On-chip Neural-ART NPU accelerator\n28\t* 4 MiB on-chip AXISRAM (no internal flash)\n29\t* External XSPI NOR flash interface\n30\t* On-board ST-LINK-V3EC\n31\t* 3 user LEDs (LD5 red, LD6 green, LD7 blue), plus status LEDs\n32\t  (LD1 5V_PWR, LD4 PWR, LD9 COM)\n33\t* User pushbutton (B1) and reset pushbutton\n34\t* 32.768 kHz crystal oscillator\n35\t* USB Type-C connectors (ST-LINK and user)\n36\t* ST Zio connector\n37\t* ST morpho headers\n38\t\n39\t.. warning::\n40\t\n41\t   This is the initial NuttX port for the STM32N6 family. The supported\n42\t   peripheral set is intentionally minimal: USART1 (the ST-LINK VCOM\n43\t   console), GPIO, RCC, PWR, the SysTick-based timer, and the three\n44\t   on-board user LEDs. Other on-chip peripherals and on-board features\n45\t   (XSPI flash boot, networking, user button, USB, MIPI, NPU, etc) are\n46\t   not yet wired up. The CPU is currently clocked at 200 MHz from PLL1.\n47\t   Raising it to the standard 600 / 800 MHz operating points is deferred\n48\t   to a follow-up change.\n49\t\n50\tButtons and LEDs\n51\t================\n52\t\n53\tThe board exposes three user LEDs and a user pushbutton:\n54\t\n55\t===== ======= ======== ======================================\n56\tID    Color   GPIO     Notes\n57\t===== ======= ======== ======================================\n58\tLD5   Red     PG10     Active low\n59\tLD6   Green   PG0      Active low\n60\tLD7   Blue    PG8      Active low\n61\tB1    Blue    PC13     Active high, external pull-down\n62\t===== ======= ======== ======================================\n63\t\n64\tThe LEDs are supported via two mutually-exclusive paths:\n65\t\n66\t* ``CONFIG_ARCH_LEDS=y`` (default when the board is selected) lets\n67\t  NuttX drive the LEDs to indicate OS state (boot stages, idle,\n68\t  interrupt activity, assertion, panic).  See\n69\t  ``boards/arm/stm32n6/nucleo-n657x0-q/include/board.h`` for the\n70\t  state-to-LED mapping.\n71\t\n72\t* ``CONFIG_ARCH_LEDS=n`` plus ``CONFIG_USERLED=y`` and\n73\t  ``CONFIG_USERLED_LOWER=y`` hands the LEDs to userspace via the\n74\t  ``/dev/userleds`` character device.  The ``nucleo-n657x0-q:leds``\n75\t  configuration bundles this with ``apps/examples/leds`` so the\n76\t  ``leds`` NSH command can cycle the LEDs as a quick smoke test.\n77\t\n78\tThe user pushbutton is not yet wired up; a polled or EXTI-based\n79\tdriver is deferred to a follow-up change.\n80\t\n81\tPin Mapping\n82\t===========\n83\t\n84\tThe shipped configurations map only the pins required for the serial\n85\tconsole. All other GPIOs retain their reset state and are free for\n86\tapplication use.\n87\t\n88\t===== ============= ======= =================================\n89\tPin   Signal        AF      Notes\n90\t===== ============= ======= =================================\n91\tPE5   USART1_TX     AF7     Routed to ST-LINK VCOM (host RX)\n92\tPE6   USART1_RX     AF7     Routed to ST-LINK VCOM (host TX)\n93\t===== ============= ======= =================================\n94\t\n95\tPower Supply\n96\t============\n97\t\n98\tThe board is powered over either USB Type-C connector (ST-LINK or\n99\tuser) at 5 V. On-board regulators derive the MCU and I/O rails. See\n100\tthe ST user manual for the full power tree and jumper selection.\n101\t\n102\tInstallation\n103\t============\n104\t\n105\tTwo host tools are required:\n106\t\n107\t* A bare-metal Arm toolchain, e.g. the GNU Arm Embedded toolchain\n108\t  (``arm-none-eabi-gcc``) packaged by most Linux distributions or\n109\t  available from Arm.\n110\t* `STM32CubeProgrammer\n111\t  <https://www.st.com/en/development-tools/stm32cubeprog.html>`_ for\n112\t  loading images into AXISRAM over the on-board ST-LINK.\n113\t\n114\tBuilding NuttX\n115\t==============\n116\t\n117\tFrom the top of the NuttX source tree:\n118\t\n119\t.. code:: console\n120\t\n121\t   $ ./tools/configure.sh nucleo-n657x0-q:<config>\n122\t   $ make\n123\t\n124\tAt the end of the build, ``nuttx.bin`` is the raw image that the\n125\tflashing step below loads into AXISRAM.\n126\t\n127\tThe toolchain selection can be changed via ``make menuconfig``.\n128\t\n129\tFlashing\n130\t========\n131\t\n132\tThe board boots in development mode: the on-board ST-LINK loads the\n133\timage directly into AXISRAM at ``0x34000400`` (the first 1 KiB is\n134\treserved for the boot ROM header) and starts execution there. Signed\n135\tXSPI flash boot via a first-stage bootloader is not yet supported.\n136\t\n137\tUse STM32CubeProgrammer's CLI to load and run ``nuttx.bin``:\n138\t\n139\t.. code:: console\n140\t\n141\t   $ STM32_Programmer_CLI -c port=SWD mode=UR -halt              \\\n142\t         -d nuttx.bin 0x34000400                                 \\\n143\t         -w32 0xE000ED08 0x34000400                              \\\n144\t         -g 0x34000400\n145\t\n146\tThe ``-w32`` write retargets VTOR to the SRAM image before the ``-g``\n147\tjump, so the Cortex-M55 starts from the NuttX vector table rather\n148\tthan the boot ROM's.\n149\t\n150\tConfigurations\n151\t==============\n152\t\n153\tEach configuration is selected with::\n154\t\n155\t   $ ./tools/configure.sh nucleo-n657x0-q:<config>\n156\t\n157\tUnless otherwise noted, console output is accessed via ST-LINK\n158\t(USART1) at 115200 8N1.\n159\t\n160\tnsh\n161\t---\n162\t\n163\tMinimal NuttShell configuration. Boots into the NSH prompt via\n164\tST-LINK and exposes the built-in commands plus\n165\t:doc:`getprime </applications/testing/getprime/index>`-style apps\n166\tselectable via ``make menuconfig``.\n167\t\n168\tostest\n169\t------\n170\t\n171\tBuilds the NSH configuration with :doc:`apps/testing/ostest\n172\t</applications/testing/ostest/index>` added. Run ``ostest`` from the\n173\tNSH prompt to exercise the core RTOS primitives (tasks, mutexes,\n174\tsemaphores, signals, message queues, POSIX timers, condition\n175\tvariables, scheduling). Used as the smoke test.\n176\t\n177\tleds\n178\t----\n179\t\n180\tBuilds the NSH configuration with ``CONFIG_ARCH_LEDS`` disabled and\n181\tthe userled lower-half driver and ``apps/examples/leds`` enabled, so\n182\tthe three on-board LEDs are exposed at ``/dev/userleds`` and can be\n183\texercised from userspace.  Run ``leds`` from the NSH prompt to spawn\n184\tthe daemon that cycles through the LEDs.\n185\t\n186\tLicense Exceptions\n187\t==================\n188\t\n189\tNone.\n190\t\n191\tReferences\n192\t==========\n193\t\n194\t* [RM0486] STM32N647/657xx Arm\\ :sup:`®`-based 32-bit MCUs reference manual\n195\t* [UM3417] STM32N6 Nucleo-144 board (MB1940) user manual\n196\t* [ES0620] STM32N657 errata sheet\n197\t"}]},"uuid":"56dc64b5-564c-4f44-86b1-8e156f588e6b","timestamp":"2026-08-20T09:19:52.737Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/warner/openvela/nuttx/Documentation/platforms/arm/stm32n6/boards/nucleo-n657x0-q/index.rst","content":"==================\nST Nucleo-N657X0-Q\n==================\n\n.. tags:: chip:stm32, chip:stm32n6, chip:stm32n657\n\n.. figure:: nucleo-n657x0-q.jpg\n   :width: 50 %\n   :align: center\n   :alt: STMicro Nucleo-N657X0-Q development board\n\n   Nucleo-N657X0-Q\n\nThe STM Nucleo-N657X0-Q is a development\nboard for the STM32N657X0H3Q, an Arm Cortex-M55 with 4 MiB of on-chip\nAXISRAM, an on-chip Neural-ART NPU, and no internal flash. In\nproduction, code is fetched from external XSPI NOR flash and in\ndevelopment, the image is loaded directly into AXISRAM by the on-board\nST-LINK-V3EC. Refer to the\nhttps://www.st.com/en/evaluation-tools/nucleo-n657x0-q.html website\nfor the full product page.\n\nFeatures\n========\n\n* STM32N657X0H3Q MCU (Arm Cortex-M55, up to 800 MHz)\n* On-chip Neural-ART NPU accelerator\n* 4 MiB on-chip AXISRAM (no internal flash)\n* External XSPI NOR flash interface\n* On-board ST-LINK-V3EC\n* 3 user LEDs (LD5 red, LD6 green, LD7 blue), plus status LEDs\n  (LD1 5V_PWR, LD4 PWR, LD9 COM)\n* User pushbutton (B1) and reset pushbutton\n* 32.768 kHz crystal oscillator\n* USB Type-C connectors (ST-LINK and user)\n* ST Zio connector\n* ST morpho headers\n\n.. warning::\n\n   This is the initial NuttX port for the STM32N6 family. The supported\n   peripheral set is intentionally minimal: USART1 (the ST-LINK VCOM\n   console), GPIO, RCC, PWR, the SysTick-based timer, and the three\n   on-board user LEDs. Other on-chip peripherals and on-board features\n   (XSPI flash boot, networking, user button, USB, MIPI, NPU, etc) are\n   not yet wired up. The CPU is currently clocked at 200 MHz from PLL1.\n   Raising it to the standard 600 / 800 MHz operating points is deferred\n   to a follow-up change.\n\nButtons and LEDs\n================\n\nThe board exposes three user LEDs and a user pushbutton:\n\n===== ======= ======== ======================================\nID    Color   GPIO     Notes\n===== ======= ======== ======================================\nLD5   Red     PG10     Active low\nLD6   Green   PG0      Active low\nLD7   Blue    PG8      Active low\nB1    Blue    PC13     Active high, external pull-down\n===== ======= ======== ======================================\n\nThe LEDs are supported via two mutually-exclusive paths:\n\n* ``CONFIG_ARCH_LEDS=y`` (default when the board is selected) lets\n  NuttX drive the LEDs to indicate OS state (boot stages, idle,\n  interrupt activity, assertion, panic).  See\n  ``boards/arm/stm32n6/nucleo-n657x0-q/include/board.h`` for the\n  state-to-LED mapping.\n\n* ``CONFIG_ARCH_LEDS=n`` plus ``CONFIG_USERLED=y`` and\n  ``CONFIG_USERLED_LOWER=y`` hands the LEDs to userspace via the\n  ``/dev/userleds`` character device.  The ``nucleo-n657x0-q:leds``\n  configuration bundles this with ``apps/examples/leds`` so the\n  ``leds`` NSH command can cycle the LEDs as a quick smoke test.\n\nThe user pushbutton is not yet wired up; a polled or EXTI-based\ndriver is deferred to a follow-up change.\n\nPin Mapping\n===========\n\nThe shipped configurations map only the pins required for the serial\nconsole. All other GPIOs retain their reset state and are free for\napplication use.\n\n===== ============= ======= =================================\nPin   Signal        AF      Notes\n===== ============= ======= =================================\nPE5   USART1_TX     AF7     Routed to ST-LINK VCOM (host RX)\nPE6   USART1_RX     AF7     Routed to ST-LINK VCOM (host TX)\n===== ============= ======= =================================\n\nPower Supply\n============\n\nThe board is powered over either USB Type-C connector (ST-LINK or\nuser) at 5 V. On-board regulators derive the MCU and I/O rails. See\nthe ST user manual for the full power tree and jumper selection.\n\nInstallation\n============\n\nTwo host tools are required:\n\n* A bare-metal Arm toolchain, e.g. the GNU Arm Embedded toolchain\n  (``arm-none-eabi-gcc``) packaged by most Linux distributions or\n  available from Arm.\n* `STM32CubeProgrammer\n  <https://www.st.com/en/development-tools/stm32cubeprog.html>`_ for\n  loading images into AXISRAM over the on-board ST-LINK.\n\nBuilding NuttX\n==============\n\nFrom the top of the NuttX source tree:\n\n.. code:: console\n\n   $ ./tools/configure.sh nucleo-n657x0-q:<config>\n   $ make\n\nAt the end of the build, ``nuttx.bin`` is the raw image that the\nflashing step below loads into AXISRAM.\n\nThe toolchain selection can be changed via ``make menuconfig``.\n\nFlashing\n========\n\nThe board boots in development mode: the on-board ST-LINK loads the\nimage directly into AXISRAM at ``0x34000400`` (the first 1 KiB is\nreserved for the boot ROM header) and starts execution there. Signed\nXSPI flash boot via a first-stage bootloader is not yet supported.\n\nUse STM32CubeProgrammer's CLI to load and run ``nuttx.bin``:\n\n.. code:: console\n\n   $ STM32_Programmer_CLI -c port=SWD mode=UR -halt              \\\n         -d nuttx.bin 0x34000400                                 \\\n         -w32 0xE000ED08 0x34000400                              \\\n         -g 0x34000400\n\nThe ``-w32`` write retargets VTOR to the SRAM image before the ``-g``\njump, so the Cortex-M55 starts from the NuttX vector table rather\nthan the boot ROM's.\n\nConfigurations\n==============\n\nEach configuration is selected with::\n\n   $ ./tools/configure.sh nucleo-n657x0-q:<config>\n\nUnless otherwise noted, console output is accessed via ST-LINK\n(USART1) at 115200 8N1.\n\nnsh\n---\n\nMinimal NuttShell configuration. Boots into the NSH prompt via\nST-LINK and exposes the built-in commands plus\n:doc:`getprime </applications/testing/getprime/index>`-style apps\nselectable via ``make menuconfig``.\n\nostest\n------\n\nBuilds the NSH configuration with :doc:`apps/testing/ostest\n</applications/testing/ostest/index>` added. Run ``ostest`` from the\nNSH prompt to exercise the core RTOS primitives (tasks, mutexes,\nsemaphores, signals, message queues, POSIX timers, condition\nvariables, scheduling). Used as the smoke test.\n\nleds\n----\n\nBuilds the NSH configuration with ``CONFIG_ARCH_LEDS`` disabled and\nthe userled lower-half driver and ``apps/examples/leds`` enabled, so\nthe three on-board LEDs are exposed at ``/dev/userleds`` and can be\nexercised from userspace.  Run ``leds`` from the NSH prompt to spawn\nthe daemon that cycles through the LEDs.\n\nLicense Exceptions\n==================\n\nNone.\n\nReferences\n==========\n\n* [RM0486] STM32N647/657xx Arm\\ :sup:`®`-based 32-bit MCUs reference manual\n* [UM3417] STM32N6 Nucleo-144 board (MB1940) user manual\n* [ES0620] STM32N657 errata sheet\n","numLines":197,"startLine":1,"totalLines":197}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c625d90f-22d9-4952-a20e-87e9e1034841","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/warner/openvela","sessionId":"4e5d5a04-986e-4343-ae7e-1f641f6267d8","version":"2.1.237","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
